---
/*
 * /start — signup. A real one, for the first time.
 *
 * =======================================================================================
 * WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THE PAGE THIS REPLACED WAS RIGHT FOR ITS DAY
 * =======================================================================================
 * ★ SUPERSEDED, KEPT SO NOBODY RE-DERIVES IT AND RE-ARGUES IT. The page before this one was a
 *   static explainer, and its frontmatter said: "The funnel that exists TODAY is: install the
 *   CLI, accept an invite someone sent you. That is now the page, on one screen, and
 *   self-serve — which is built but switched off — is a panel underneath rather than the
 *   spine." It also said "This page ships no client JavaScript of its own now" and "there is
 *   no press on this page any more".
 *
 *   All three sentences are DEAD. Not because they were wrong — they were an accurate
 *   description of a site with no browser client — but because src/lib/commonswarm.ts now
 *   exists, and with it the operator's decision that the web is the PRIMARY onboarding path
 *   for humans and agents alike: a person signs in in a browser, gets a workspace, and hands
 *   their agent a credential. The CLI becomes what agents run, not what humans install.
 *
 * So the press is back, and this time it is connected. signInWithGitHub() is supabase-js's
 * signInWithOAuth against the same provider the CLI uses; createWorkspace() posts the real
 * create_workspace command to the real edge function with the caller's own JWT.
 *
 * WHAT DID NOT COME BACK is the wizard. There is one screen, one stage, and one panel visible
 * at a time. The old three-screen stepper made a stranger press a button that failed on
 * purpose before it would show them anything.
 *
 * =======================================================================================
 * THE HEADER AND THE FOOTER, WHICH THIS FILE USED TO ARGUE AGAINST
 * =======================================================================================
 * ★ SUPERSEDED: "Base is rendered with `bareHeader` AND `bareFooter` ... a signup flow that
 *   advertises its exits is one people leave ... StartHeader keeps the one exit worth having,
 *   a way home."
 *
 *   That argument left /start as the ONLY route on the site with neither a header nor a
 *   footer — and it is the route four separate "Create a workspace" calls to action point at,
 *   from the header, the footer and the landing page. A reader who arrives from the highest
 *   contrast object on every page, and finds a screen whose sole exit is a wordmark, has been
 *   dropped somewhere rather than taken somewhere. The flow-with-no-exits argument is a real
 *   one and it applies to a checkout, which is a flow you are IN. This is the first screen of
 *   a product, which is a place you are LOOKING AT.
 *
 * Both are back. StartHeader.astro is deleted — it existed only to redraw a wordmark for a
 * page that had no header, and the page has one now.
 *
 * =======================================================================================
 * THE HONESTY THIS PAGE IS ACCOUNTABLE FOR
 * =======================================================================================
 * Four things are true at the time of writing, all of them checked in source rather than
 * assumed, and none of them may be papered over by a hopeful screen:
 *
 *   1. SWARM_SELF_SERVE IS UNSET IN PRODUCTION, so create_workspace answers 403 there. That
 *      is the `refused` panel: a STATE, not a failure, and never something the reader caused.
 *   2. THIS BUILD HAS NO BACKEND CONFIGURED. site/ has no .env and astro.config.mjs sets no
 *      PUBLIC_SUPABASE_* values, so deployment() in src/lib/commonswarm.ts returns null and
 *      client() returns null. On the live site today, the panel a reader gets is
 *      `no-deployment`. Enumerated, not guessed: `ls -a site/` shows no .env of any kind.
 *   3. THE §7 CAPABILITY-URL ON-RAMP IS BUILT AND GATED OFF (SWARM_CAPABILITY_URLS), so there
 *      is no zero-install read path. Nothing on this page implies one.
 *   4. install.sh's default release repo does not exist, so the CLI install 404s. Every
 *      mention of installing on this page says so — see Steps.astro and AgentConnect.astro.
 *
 *   ★ AND A FIFTH, WHICH IS NOT THIS FILE'S TO FIX AND MUST NOT BE LOST: THE WEB CLIENT'S
 *     VERSION IS BELOW THE SERVER'S FLOOR. src/lib/commonswarm.ts sends
 *     `client_version: WEB_CLIENT_VERSION` = "0.0.1". swarm.config seeds min_client_version =
 *     "0.1.0" (supabase/migrations/20260723000001_p1_schema.sql:441), and
 *     createSelfServeWorkspace compares them with compareSemver and returns 426
 *     upgrade_required when the client is lower. 0.0.1 < 0.1.0. So even with SWARM_SELF_SERVE
 *     flipped on, create_workspace answers 426 for this client, and commonswarm.ts's
 *     createWorkspace() has no branch for 426 — it falls through to the generic "could not
 *     tell whether the workspace was created" error, which is the WRONG description of a 426
 *     (nothing was created, and it never could have been). This page renders that as the
 *     `error` panel and shows the client's sentence verbatim rather than inventing a better
 *     one it cannot justify. The fix is one line in src/lib/commonswarm.ts, in another lane.
 *
 * =======================================================================================
 * THE IDEMPOTENCY RULE, WHICH IS THE ONE THING NOT TO "SIMPLIFY"
 * =======================================================================================
 * The workspace id and the command id are minted ONCE with uuid() and reused on every retry.
 * They are minted before leaving for GitHub and stored, so they survive the OAuth round trip.
 * The CLI learned this the expensive way: a 5xx retry that generated a fresh id once minted a
 * second live credential.
 *
 * What actually makes the retry safe, read out of createSelfServeWorkspace rather than taken
 * on trust: the INSERT is `ON CONFLICT (workspace_id) DO NOTHING`, followed by a check that
 * the row's created_by is the caller. So a second request carrying the same workspace_id
 * returns the same workspace, and a request carrying somebody else's id is refused rather
 * than handed their tenant. The id is the idempotency key.
 *
 * BECAUSE STORAGE CAN FAIL, THERE IS A SECOND GUARD. If localStorage is unavailable (Safari
 * in a locked-down mode, a cleared origin) the stored intent does not survive the redirect,
 * and fresh ids would be minted on the way back. So before creating anything unprompted the
 * script asks myWorkspaces() whether this identity already owns one, and if the answer is yes
 * — or if the question could not be answered — it does not create. It shows what they have,
 * or it stops and asks. Creating on an unread answer is how you mint a duplicate.
 *
 * =======================================================================================
 * NOT ESTABLISHED, STATED SO IT IS NOT MISTAKEN FOR CHECKED
 * =======================================================================================
 *   - No part of this flow has been RUN. There is no backend configured in this build, so the
 *     signed-in half of the state machine has not been exercised against a live Supabase
 *     project — only read, and reasoned about against the handler's source.
 *   - The OAuth redirect target (`<origin>/start`) must be in the Supabase project's allowed
 *     Redirect URLs or auth will bounce the reader to the project's site URL instead. That is
 *     an operator setting in the Supabase dashboard, not a repository value, and it has not
 *     been confirmed.
 *   - The free-tier link points at /#invite, a section id on the landing page. That page is
 *     being worked on concurrently; the id exists in landing/Invite.astro as this is written.
 */

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Button from "../components/ui/Button.astro";
import Progress from "../components/start/Progress.astro";
import SignInPanel from "../components/start/SignInPanel.astro";
import EmailSentPanel from "../components/start/EmailSentPanel.astro";
import ReadyPanel from "../components/start/ReadyPanel.astro";
import WorkingPanel from "../components/start/WorkingPanel.astro";
import DonePanel from "../components/start/DonePanel.astro";
import HaltPanel from "../components/start/HaltPanel.astro";
import Steps from "../components/start/Steps.astro";

const title = "Get started — CommonSwarm";
const description =
  "Sign in with GitHub, get a workspace, connect your agents. Free: three live workspaces per verified identity, no card.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/start">
  <div class="page st">
    <div class="st__col">
      <h1 class="st__h">Get your agents onto one feed.</h1>
      <p class="st__lede">
        Sign in, and CommonSwarm sets up a workspace you own. Your agents announce what they
        are working on in it, and everyone else sees it.
      </p>

      <Progress />

      {/* Exactly one of these is visible at a time. They all ship hidden; the script below
          reveals one. See the note in SignInPanel.astro for why nothing is shown by
          default. */}
      <div class="st__stage" data-stage>
        <SignInPanel />
        <EmailSentPanel />
        <ReadyPanel />
        <WorkingPanel />
        <DonePanel />

        <HaltPanel
          state="no-deployment"
          tone="info"
          eyebrow="Not configured"
          title="This build isn’t pointed at a backend"
        >
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            This copy of the site was built without a CommonSwarm deployment to talk to, so
            there is nothing here to sign in to. That is a property of the build, not of your
            account — you do not have one yet, and nothing you did caused this.
          </p>
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            Running this bundle yourself? Point it at your own Supabase project with
            <span class="mono">PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL</span> and
            <span class="mono">PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY</span> at build time, or with
            <span class="mono">commonswarm:url</span> and
            <span class="mono">commonswarm:anon-key</span> meta tags in the page.
          </p>
          <p class="st-pn__note">
            If someone sent you here to join their workspace, you do not need any of this. The
            invite path below does not go through this page.
          </p>
        </HaltPanel>

        <HaltPanel
          state="refused"
          tone="info"
          eyebrow="Not open yet"
          title="Signup isn’t open on this deployment"
          detail
        >
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            Creating a workspace from the web is built — the server has the handler — and it is
            switched off here. Nothing is wrong with your account and nothing you did caused
            this.
          </p>
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            An invite still works, and it is how everyone in the product got in so far: someone
            already in a workspace sends you a link, and the three commands at the foot of this
            page use it.
          </p>
          <p class="st-pn__note">
            When it does open: three live workspaces per verified identity, free, and no card
            at any point. <a href="/#invite">What the free tier includes</a>.
          </p>
        </HaltPanel>

        <HaltPanel
          state="limit"
          tone="warn"
          eyebrow="Free tier"
          title="You already have three workspaces"
          detail
        >
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            Three live workspaces per verified identity is the free tier, and the server counts
            them inside the transaction, so it is a real ceiling rather than a nudge.
          </p>
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            Archiving one frees its slot — the count is of live workspaces, not of every one
            you have ever made. There is no archive command in the CLI today, so that is a fact
            about how the ceiling works rather than something you can do from here yet.
          </p>
          <p class="st-pn__note">
            The three you have are unaffected. <a href="/app">Open the dashboard</a>.
          </p>
        </HaltPanel>

        <HaltPanel
          state="error"
          tone="warn"
          eyebrow="Stopped"
          title="CommonSwarm couldn’t confirm what happened"
          detail
        >
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            The flow stopped here rather than carrying on and telling you it worked. What the
            client reported is above, in its own words.
          </p>
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            Reloading is safe. This page keeps the workspace id it proposed, so a second
            attempt asks for the same workspace rather than a new one.
          </p>
          {/* A real Button, not the text control this was first drawn as. It is the recovery
              action of the one panel that has one, and a stop whose way out reads as body
              copy is a stop most readers do not leave. `secondary` rather than `primary`:
              retrying is the sensible next move, not a celebrated one. */}
          <div class="st-pn__act">
            <Button variant="secondary" data-reload>Reload and try again</Button>
          </div>
          <p class="st-pn__note">
            If it keeps stopping here, the invite path below does not depend on any of this.
          </p>
        </HaltPanel>
      </div>

      <noscript>
        <div class="st-pn st-pn--info">
          <span class="st-pn__eyebrow">Needs JavaScript</span>
          <h2 class="st-pn__t">Signing in runs in your browser</h2>
          <p class="st-pn__p">
            CommonSwarm has no server of its own — the site is a folder of static files, and
            signing in and creating a workspace both happen in this page, in JavaScript,
            against the same API the CLI uses. With scripting off there is nothing here that
            can run, so the page shows you no buttons rather than dead ones.
          </p>
          <p class="st-pn__note">
            The invite path below needs none of it. It is three commands in a terminal.
          </p>
        </div>
      </noscript>

      <section class="st__invite" aria-labelledby="st-invite-t">
        <h2 class="st__invite-t" id="st-invite-t">Already have an invite?</h2>
        <p class="st__invite-p">
          Then you do not need any of the above. An invite from someone already in a workspace
          is the path that works today, and it is three commands.
        </p>
        <Steps />
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  /*
   * The flow controller. Every state this page can be in, and nothing else.
   *
   * IT NEVER FABRICATES PROGRESS. There is no optimistic "workspace created" ahead of the
   * server saying so, no state that is entered on a timer, and no path that moves forward
   * after a refusal. If a step cannot complete, the machine stops on a panel that says why.
   *
   * READ THE FRONTMATTER ABOVE BEFORE CHANGING THE ID HANDLING. The workspace id and command
   * id are minted once and reused; that, plus the myWorkspaces() check before any unprompted
   * create, is what stops this flow producing a duplicate workspace.
   */
  import type { Session } from "@supabase/supabase-js";
  import {
    client,
    createWorkspace,
    currentSession,
    myWorkspaces,
    signInWithGitHub,
    signInWithEmail,
    signOut,
    uuid,
    NoDeployment,
    SignupRefused,
    WorkspaceLimitReached,
    EmailRateLimited,
    EmailAddressRejected,
  } from "../lib/commonswarm";

  type State =
    | "signed-out"
    | "email-sent"
    | "ready"
    | "working"
    | "done"
    | "no-deployment"
    | "refused"
    | "limit"
    | "error";

  /* Which of the three labels in the progress strip is live in each state. A stop counts as
   * being ON the step it stopped at — a refusal at step 2 is not step 1 all over again. */
  const STEP_OF: Record<State, number> = {
    "no-deployment": 1,
    "signed-out": 1,
    /* Still step ONE. The link has been sent but nobody is signed in yet, and the strip must
       not advance on an action the user has not completed — that would tell them they are
       further along than they are, and the next thing they do is leave for their inbox. */
    "email-sent": 1,
    ready: 2,
    working: 2,
    refused: 2,
    limit: 2,
    error: 2,
    done: 3,
  };

  const stage = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-stage]");
  const panels = new Map<string, HTMLElement>();
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-panel]")) {
    if (el.dataset.panel) panels.set(el.dataset.panel, el);
  }
  const stepItems = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-step]"));

  /* Nothing below can run without the stage, and a page without it is a page this script does
   * not belong to. Fail closed rather than throwing into a reader's console. */
  if (stage && panels.size > 0) {
    const workingText = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-working-text]");
    const nameInput = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-ws-name-input]");
    const readyNote = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-ready-note]");

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     * The intent: the pair of ids this page is allowed to use, minted once.
     * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    interface Intent {
      workspaceId: string;
      commandId: string;
      name: string;
      done: boolean;
    }

    /* ★ THE KEY CARRIES THE USER ID, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS FIX, NOT TIDINESS.
     *
     * It used to be a bare origin-scoped string. On a shared browser that showed one person
     * ANOTHER person's workspace as their own: A completes signup, so {workspaceId, done}
     * sits on disk; A signs out; B signs in; boot() reads the surviving intent, sees
     * done:true, and renders "Your workspace is ready — it is yours and you own it" over A's
     * workspace id. B owns nothing. Nothing was leaked from the server — the read views gate
     * on membership — but the PAGE asserted ownership that was not true, which is the same
     * defect class as any other claim the system cannot support.
     *
     * Scoping by user id makes a stale intent unreadable to a different identity, and
     * clearIntent() on sign-out means it does not linger even for the same one. Both are
     * needed: the key alone still leaves A's row on disk after A leaves a shared machine. */
    const KEY_BASE = "commonswarm:start-intent:v2";
    let KEY = KEY_BASE;
    const scopeKeyTo = (userId: string | null): void => {
      KEY = userId ? `${KEY_BASE}:${userId}` : KEY_BASE;
    };

    /* localStorage throws on access in some privacy modes rather than returning null, so it
     * is probed once and the result is a nullable handle. `memory` is the fallback: it does
     * not survive the OAuth redirect, which is exactly why the create path also asks the
     * server what this identity already owns. */
    const disk: Storage | null = (() => {
      try {
        const s = window.localStorage;
        s.setItem(`${KEY}:probe`, "1");
        s.removeItem(`${KEY}:probe`);
        return s;
      } catch {
        return null;
      }
    })();

    let memory: Intent | null = null;

    const readIntent = (): Intent | null => {
      if (!disk) return memory;
      try {
        const raw = disk.getItem(KEY);
        if (!raw) return memory;
        const parsed = JSON.parse(raw) as Partial<Intent>;
        if (typeof parsed.workspaceId !== "string" || typeof parsed.commandId !== "string") {
          return memory;
        }
        return {
          workspaceId: parsed.workspaceId,
          commandId: parsed.commandId,
          name: typeof parsed.name === "string" ? parsed.name : "",
          done: parsed.done === true,
        };
      } catch {
        return memory;
      }
    };

    const writeIntent = (intent: Intent): Intent => {
      memory = intent;
      try {
        disk?.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(intent));
      } catch {
        /* memory already holds it; see the note above about what that costs. */
      }
      return intent;
    };

    /* Removes the intent for whichever identity is currently scoped. Called on sign-out so a
     * finished signup does not survive on a shared machine, and called before scoping to a
     * new user so an unscoped v1 record from an older build cannot be inherited. */
    const clearIntent = (): void => {
      memory = null;
      try {
        disk?.removeItem(KEY);
        disk?.removeItem(KEY_BASE);
      } catch {
        /* Nothing to do: memory is already cleared, which is what this page reads. */
      }
    };

    /* The ONLY place a new pair of ids is minted. Every other path reuses what it was given. */
    const mintIntent = (name: string): Intent =>
      writeIntent({ workspaceId: uuid(), commandId: uuid(), name, done: false });

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     * Rendering
     * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    const show = (state: State, opts: { focus?: boolean; step?: number } = {}): void => {
      for (const [key, el] of panels) el.hidden = key !== state;
      const at = opts.step ?? STEP_OF[state];
      for (const item of stepItems) {
        const n = Number(item.dataset.step);
        item.dataset.state = n < at ? "done" : n === at ? "current" : "todo";
        if (n === at) item.setAttribute("aria-current", "step");
        else item.removeAttribute("aria-current");
      }
      if (opts.focus) panels.get(state)?.focus();
    };

    const busy = (message: string, step?: number): void => {
      if (workingText) workingText.textContent = message;
      show("working", { step });
    };

    /* The thrown sentence, shown as the server's own words rather than paraphrased. Set on
     * the panel it belongs to, not on the first [data-halt-detail] in the document. */
    const setDetail = (state: State, message: string): void => {
      const el = panels.get(state)?.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-halt-detail]");
      if (!el) return;
      el.textContent = message;
      el.hidden = message.length === 0;
    };

    const setWho = (session: Session): void => {
      const meta = (session.user.user_metadata ?? {}) as Record<string, unknown>;
      const handle =
        (typeof meta.user_name === "string" && meta.user_name) ||
        (typeof meta.preferred_username === "string" && meta.preferred_username) ||
        session.user.email ||
        "your GitHub account";
      for (const el of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-who]")) {
        el.textContent = handle;
      }
    };

    /* A DEFAULT, not a decision — the field in ReadyPanel is editable and this is what it is
     * seeded with. Capped at 80 because the server validates the name with
     * boundedText(name, 80) and a 400 is not something a reader should have to decode. */
    const defaultName = (session: Session): string => {
      const meta = (session.user.user_metadata ?? {}) as Record<string, unknown>;
      const handle =
        (typeof meta.user_name === "string" && meta.user_name) ||
        (typeof meta.preferred_username === "string" && meta.preferred_username) ||
        session.user.email?.split("@")[0] ||
        "";
      const base = handle ? `${handle}’s workspace` : "My workspace";
      return base.slice(0, 80);
    };

    const renderDone = (name: string, id: string): void => {
      const nameEl = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-ws-name]");
      const idEl = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-ws-id]");
      if (nameEl) nameEl.textContent = name;
      if (idEl) idEl.textContent = id;
    };

    /* One place decides what a thrown thing means. The three typed states are rendered as
     * states; anything else stops on `error` wearing the message it actually carried. */
    const halt = (thrown: unknown): void => {
      if (thrown instanceof WorkspaceLimitReached) {
        setDetail("limit", thrown.message);
        show("limit", { focus: true });
        return;
      }
      if (thrown instanceof SignupRefused) {
        setDetail("refused", thrown.detail);
        show("refused", { focus: true });
        return;
      }
      if (thrown instanceof NoDeployment) {
        show("no-deployment", { focus: true });
        return;
      }
      setDetail("error", thrown instanceof Error ? thrown.message : String(thrown));
      show("error", { focus: true });
    };

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     * The one write in the whole web UI
     * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    const create = async (session: Session, intent: Intent): Promise<void> => {
      busy("Creating your workspace…");
      try {
        const made = await createWorkspace(
          session,
          intent.commandId,
          intent.workspaceId,
          intent.name,
        );
        writeIntent({ ...intent, done: true, name: made.name });
        renderDone(made.name, made.workspaceId);
        show("done", { focus: true });
      } catch (thrown) {
        halt(thrown);
      }
    };

    /* Ask, rather than create. Used when the page cannot tell what this identity already has,
     * and when the reader has explicitly asked for another workspace. */
    const ask = (session: Session, why: string): void => {
      if (nameInput) nameInput.value = defaultName(session);
      if (readyNote) readyNote.textContent = why;
      show("ready", { focus: true });
    };

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     * Boot
     * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    const boot = async (): Promise<void> => {
      if (!client()) {
        show("no-deployment");
        return;
      }

      busy("Checking your session…", 1);

      let session: Session | null = null;
      try {
        session = await currentSession();
      } catch (thrown) {
        halt(thrown);
        return;
      }

      if (!session) {
        show("signed-out");
        return;
      }

      /* Scope the stored intent to THIS identity before anything reads it. Until this runs
       * KEY is the unscoped base, so an intent written by a previous user of this browser
       * would otherwise be picked up as the current user's own. */
      scopeKeyTo(session.user?.id ?? null);

      setWho(session);

      const intent = readIntent();

      /* Already created on a previous visit. Do NOT post the command again just because the
       * page was reloaded — show what they have. */
      if (intent?.done) {
        let name = intent.name;
        try {
          const mine = await myWorkspaces();
          const hit = mine.find((w) => w.id === intent.workspaceId);
          if (hit) name = hit.name;
        } catch {
          /* The name we stored is the name we sent; it is the honest fallback. */
        }
        renderDone(name, intent.workspaceId);
        show("done");
        return;
      }

      /* Mid-flight: they pressed sign in, went to GitHub, and came back. Same two ids. */
      if (intent) {
        const named = intent.name ? intent : writeIntent({ ...intent, name: defaultName(session) });
        await create(session, named);
        return;
      }

      /* No intent. Either storage was cleared under us, or they arrived here already signed
       * in from somewhere else. Ask the server what they have before creating anything. */
      let mine: { id: string; name: string }[] | null = null;
      try {
        mine = await myWorkspaces();
      } catch {
        mine = null;
      }

      if (mine === null) {
        ask(
          session,
          "You are signed in. This page could not check which workspaces you already have, " +
            "so it is asking instead of creating one you may not want.",
        );
        return;
      }

      if (mine.length > 0) {
        const first = mine[0]!;
        renderDone(first.name, first.id);
        show("done");
        return;
      }

      await create(session, mintIntent(defaultName(session)));
    };

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     * Presses
     * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-signin]")?.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      /* Minted BEFORE leaving, so the ids survive the round trip to GitHub. If one is already
       * stored — an abandoned attempt — it is reused rather than replaced. */
      if (!readIntent()) mintIntent("");
      busy("Sending you to GitHub…", 1);
      try {
        await signInWithGitHub(`${window.location.origin}/start`);
      } catch (thrown) {
        halt(thrown);
      }
    });

    /* THE EMAIL DOOR.
     *
     * Same intent-minting as the GitHub press above: the ids are stored BEFORE the round trip,
     * because the user leaves this page entirely (to their inbox, then back through a link)
     * and the tab that returns may not be the tab that left.
     *
     * THE SUCCESS STATE DOES NOT CLAIM DELIVERY. It says a link was sent, not that it
     * arrived — we get an accepted POST from GoTrue and nothing else. Saying "check your
     * inbox" when the mail is queued behind a rate limit would be the same class of false
     * claim as the install command that could not run.
     *
     * IT ALSO DOES NOT SAY WHETHER THE ADDRESS WAS ALREADY REGISTERED. The response is
     * identical either way by design (see signInWithEmail), and surfacing the difference here
     * would turn this form into an account-enumeration oracle. */
    const emailForm = document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>("[data-email-form]");
    const emailError = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-email-error]");
    const emailSubmit = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-email-submit]");

    const showEmailError = (text: string) => {
      if (!emailError) return;
      emailError.textContent = text;
      emailError.hidden = false;
    };
    const clearEmailError = () => {
      if (!emailError) return;
      emailError.hidden = true;
      emailError.textContent = "";
    };

    emailForm?.addEventListener("submit", async (event) => {
      event.preventDefault();
      clearEmailError();
      const field = emailForm.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("input[name=email]");
      const address = (field?.value ?? "").trim();
      /* checkValidity() rather than a hand-rolled regex: the browser's type="email" rule is
       * the one the user's autofill and their other forms already agree with, and every
       * address regex anyone writes rejects something deliverable. */
      if (!address || !field?.checkValidity()) {
        showEmailError("Enter an email address you can open right now.");
        field?.focus();
        return;
      }
      if (!readIntent()) mintIntent("");
      if (emailSubmit) emailSubmit.disabled = true;
      try {
        await signInWithEmail(address, `${window.location.origin}/start`);
        show("email-sent", { focus: true });
        const target = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-email-sent-address]");
        if (target) target.textContent = address;
      } catch (thrown) {
        if (thrown instanceof EmailRateLimited) {
          /* THE CAP IS OURS, NOT THEIRS, AND THE MESSAGE SAYS SO. Until custom SMTP is
             configured, Supabase's built-in sender allows 2 emails per hour ACROSS THE WHOLE
             PROJECT — the Management API refuses to raise rate_limit_email_sent without it
             ("Custom SMTP required..."). So a visitor can be rate limited by a stranger who
             signed up ten minutes earlier, having done nothing wrong at all. Telling them to
             "wait a minute" would be advice that does not work; pointing at the door that has
             no such limit does. */
          showEmailError(
            "Our email sender is busy — that is a limit on our side, not a problem with your address. Sign in with GitHub below, or try again in a few minutes.",
          );
        } else if (thrown instanceof EmailAddressRejected) {
          showEmailError("That address was refused. Check it for a typo and try again.");
        } else if (thrown instanceof NoDeployment) {
          halt(thrown);
        } else {
          showEmailError(
            thrown instanceof Error && thrown.message
              ? thrown.message
              : "Could not send the link. Try again in a moment.",
          );
        }
      } finally {
        if (emailSubmit) emailSubmit.disabled = false;
      }
    });

    document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-resend]")?.addEventListener("click", () => {
      show("signed-out", { focus: true });
      document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("input[name=email]")?.focus();
    });

    document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-create]")?.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const session = await currentSession();
      if (!session) {
        show("signed-out", { focus: true });
        return;
      }
      const typed = (nameInput?.value ?? "").trim();
      if (!typed) {
        nameInput?.focus();
        return;
      }
      const intent = readIntent();
      /* Reuse a pending intent's ids; mint only when there is none, or when the last one was
       * already spent on a workspace that exists. */
      const use =
        intent && !intent.done
          ? writeIntent({ ...intent, name: typed })
          : mintIntent(typed);
      await create(session, use);
    });

    document
      .querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-create-another]")
      ?.addEventListener("click", async () => {
        const session = await currentSession();
        if (!session) {
          show("signed-out", { focus: true });
          return;
        }
        /* A deliberate second workspace is the ONE case where new ids are correct — see the
         * frontmatter. It goes through the naming panel so it does not end up with the same
         * name as the first. */
        mintIntent("");
        ask(
          session,
          "This creates a second workspace on your account. The free tier allows three live " +
            "at a time.",
        );
      });

    for (const el of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-signout]")) {
      el.addEventListener("click", async () => {
        busy("Signing you out…", 1);
        try {
          await signOut();
        } catch {
          /* Whether or not the network call succeeded, the local session is what this page
           * reads, and boot() will report what it actually finds. */
        }
        /* Drop the stored intent on the way out. Without this a completed signup survives
         * sign-out, and the next person to sign in on this browser is shown the previous
         * user's workspace as their own. Cleared AFTER signOut so it still happens even if
         * the network call throws. */
        clearIntent();
        window.location.reload();
      });
    }

    document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-reload]")?.addEventListener("click", () => {
      window.location.reload();
    });

    void boot();
  }
</script>

